(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-benign-skin-lesions-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Benign Skin Lesions Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 2-year-old boy has a flat, blanchable, pink-purple patch on one side of his face that stops sharply at the midline. His parents say it has been there since birth, has grown in proportion with him, and is darker when he cries. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nevus simplex","Cherry angioma","Nevus flammeus","Infantile hemangioma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — present at birth, unilateral with sharp midline cutoff, no involution. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a large keratoacanthoma on the nasal tip. Which approach does the lecture indicate?","choices":["Intralesional methotrexate as definitive treatment","Mohs surgery","Standard elliptical excision with five-millimetre margins regardless of site","Cryotherapy, which spares the nasal cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is indicated for large or recurrent lesions, and for lesions in anatomic areas with cosmetic or functional considerations. This lesion is both. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a firm, movable, round nodule on his upper back. There is a small central pore, and pressing it expresses cream-coloured pasty material with an unpleasant smell. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Epidermoid cyst","Lipoma","Dermatofibroma","Pilonidal cyst"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the central punctum and the odour of the keratin contents. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 40-year-old has a raised scar and you are not certain whether it is a keloid or something else. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Obtain magnetic resonance imaging of the lesion","Diagnose clinically; biopsy only if there is real doubt","Biopsy every raised scar, because histology is the only way to be certain","Excise it immediately and send the whole specimen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — biopsy may itself induce new scarring, so the diagnostic act can provoke the disease it is investigating. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The same patient is not bothered by her lesion but wants to know her options. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Intralesional corticosteroid will flatten it over several months","Cryotherapy is the treatment of choice for pigmented dermal nodules","No treatment is needed; a shave or punch biopsy would remove it","Wide local excision is required in every case because of melanoma risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — in a small lesion that biopsy is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Larger lesions may need surgical excision instead. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What should be done for that patient beyond addressing the lesion itself?","choices":["Screen for diabetes mellitus and check a haemoglobin A1c","Check thyroid function tests","Check liver function tests and a coagulation screen","Screen for hyperlipidemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — xanthelasma is associated with lipid disorders, and may signify an increased risk of cardiac disease. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 15-year-old with moderate acne has a family history of keloids. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Wait until the acne resolves on its own before starting any treatment","Begin silicone sheeting over the acne-prone areas now","Avoid all topical acne therapy, since it provokes fibrous overgrowth","Treat the acne early and appropriately"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — acne is one of the named keloid triggers, and early appropriate treatment greatly increases the chance of scar-free healing. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"That infant is to be treated medically. Which is the first-line option?","choices":["A beta-blocker","Intralesional fluorouracil","Oral isotretinoin","Intralesional methotrexate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — oral propranolol or topical timolol, whose mechanism is not well understood. Corticosteroids are the other named first-line treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A ward sister asks how best to reduce pressure injuries among her immobile patients. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Early debridement of any area of erythema that appears","Prevention, and the deck calls it the best measure","Prophylactic antibiotics for every patient with limited mobility","Silicone gel sheeting applied over every bony prominence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — frequent skin assessment, nutrition assessment, moisture control and skin care, repositioning every two hours, pain management, improved mobility and specialty mattresses such as an air mattress. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 4-month-old has a rapidly enlarging periorbital hemangioma that is beginning to obstruct the visual axis. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue serial observation, since most lesions involute","Refer for surgical excision as the first-line option","Reassure and review in six months","Begin treatment, because functional involvement is an indication"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cosmetic concern, functional involvement, deep ulceration and infection all indicate treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Four patients each have a hard, hornlike keratotic projection. Which of them best matches the epidemiology the lecture gives for cutaneous horn?","choices":["A 3-month-old with a bright red lesion on the scalp","A 28-year-old pregnant woman with a bleeding finger lesion","A 62-year-old Caucasian with a lesion on the ear","A 14-year-old girl with lesions on the eyelids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — Caucasians over fifty, on head, neck and upper extremities. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 16-year-old is being evaluated for a lesion on the finger pad of the fourth digit of her left hand. She says it came up rapidly after she injured her finger and bleeds easily if she bumps it. Examination shows a moist, vascular, dome-shaped lesion. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cherry angioma","Telangiectasia","Syringoma","Pyogenic granuloma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — rapid growth after trauma, moist surface, and easy bleeding. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A patient has a blind-ending track opening onto the skin over the coccyx. Which term describes it, and how does it differ from the alternative?","choices":["A sinus; a fistula instead connects two epithelium-lined surfaces","A fistula; a sinus instead connects two epithelium-lined surfaces","A sinus; a fistula instead is always congenital in origin","A fistula; a sinus instead is lined with keratin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both usually arise from a preceding abscess. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A patient asks why her surgical scar has become stronger over the past year. Which explanation is most appropriate?","choices":["Progressive replacement of collagen by adipose tissue","Progressive cross-linking of collagen fibers as the scar matures","Progressive proliferation of endothelial cells within the scar","Progressive accumulation of keratin beneath the scar surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the remodeling phase of wound healing. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus has a painful corn on his fifth toe. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Excise the lesion with a five-millimetre margin","Refer him to podiatry","Start an over-the-counter salicylic acid product and review in a month","Pare the lesion down in clinic and advise wider footwear"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck flags the diabetic patient specifically for podiatry referral. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with osteoarthritis has a translucent skin-coloured papule over the distal interphalangeal joint of her index finger. The nail beyond it has a longitudinal groove. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acrochordon","Digital mucous cyst","Epidermoid cyst","Chronic paronychia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pressure on the nail matrix causes the groove. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on his trunk. They blanch with pressure and have accumulated over the last decade. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Petechiae","Cherry angiomas","Spider angiomas","Pyogenic granulomas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — acquired, increasing with age, once called senile angiomas. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"An 84-year-old nursing home resident has a reddened area over her sacrum. The skin is intact and the erythema does not blanch when you press on it. Which stage of pressure injury is this?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Deep tissue pressure injury","Unstageable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — non-blanchable erythema of intact skin. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"You are counting café au lait macules on that same 9-year-old. How many are described as diagnostic, and what caveat applies?","choices":["Six or more; and the macules alone establish the diagnosis","Three or more; but only if they appear after puberty","Six or more; but the macules alone do not establish the diagnosis","Two or more; and the macules alone establish the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — over five millimetres prepubertal, over fifteen postpubertal. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man had an appendicectomy five weeks ago. The scar is raised, firm and red but stops exactly at the edges of the original incision. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypertrophic scar","Keloid","Foreign-body granuloma","Dermatofibroma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it developed within four weeks and is confined to the wound margins. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"After drainage, the patient asks how to stop the problem recurring. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Keep the area clean and free of debris, and consider shaving or laser hair therapy","Avoid sitting for more than two hours at a time and use a specialty cushion","Apply an over-the-counter salicylic acid preparation twice weekly","Avoid ear piercing and other cosmetic procedures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and seek care promptly if an abscess occurs again. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 6-week-old former preterm girl has a bright red raised plaque on her scalp. Her mother says the area first looked pale, then developed fine red lines, and has grown noticeably in the last three weeks. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Infantile hemangioma","Nevus flammeus","Nevus simplex","Pyogenic granuloma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the earliest sign is blanching of the involved skin, then fine telangiectasias, then a red or crimson macule, then rapid proliferation. The superficial type is the commonest. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 9-year-old has eight light brown macules over 5 mm across on his trunk, freckling in both axillae, and several soft papules protruding just above the skin. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Neurofibromatosis type 1","Neurofibromatosis type 2","Multiple acrochordons","Multiple dermatofibromas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — café au lait spots, intertriginous freckling and cutaneous neurofibromas. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman develops several spider angiomas during pregnancy and asks whether she will need treatment. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["They indicate liver failure and require urgent investigation","They will fade if she avoids sun exposure","They usually resolve after delivery","They will persist for life and require laser in every case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those caused by oral contraceptives resolve after stopping the pill. No treatment may be needed, and pulsed dye laser resolves most lesions that persist. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A patient elects to have a benign lesion removed purely for appearance. What must she be told first?","choices":["That the lesion would become malignant if left in place","That lifelong surveillance of the site will be needed","The risk of pigmentary changes and the chance of recurrence","That histology is required on every excised specimen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both must be fully explained beforehand. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A newborn has a pink irregular patch on the nape of the neck that blanches with pressure and becomes much more obvious when he cries. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Infantile hemangioma","Telangiectasia","Nevus simplex","Nevus flammeus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the stork bite, a more superficial variant of nevus flammeus. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"The same patient wants the lesions removed for cosmetic reasons. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Elliptical excision with five-millimetre margins under local anesthesia","Intralesional corticosteroid injection at monthly intervals","Referral to a vascular anomalies specialist","Scissor excision, cryotherapy or electrodesiccation, without anesthesia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — anesthesia is stated not to be necessary. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"You are not certain whether one of those lesions is a basal cell carcinoma. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start light electrocautery to all lesions and review","Dermoscopy, which can distinguish the two, with biopsy if concern remains","Excise all the lesions with clear margins","Reassure, since sebaceous hyperplasia never resembles a carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — patients often present worried about exactly this. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"The same patient is not troubled by the lesion. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Surgical excision now, because of the risk of liposarcoma","Aspiration of the contents","Intralesional corticosteroid injection","Observation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — excision is for a cosmetically deforming mass or diagnostic uncertainty. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man has a sacral wound with partial-thickness skin loss. The exposed dermis is pink, viable and moist. No fat is visible. Which stage is this?","choices":["Stage 3","Stage 1","Stage 4","Stage 2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — partial thickness, viable pink-red bed, no adipose visible. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
